    <!-- Integrations Cycle 3 — the legacy ABM aggregations, as data.
         =============================================================
         Eight numbers the legacy application computed with inline arithmetic in
         `om_hr_payroll/models/hr_zoho_staging.py`, none of which existed
         anywhere a payroll officer could read them. They are rules now.

         CYCLE 8: EVERY ROW BELOW IS A SENTENCE NOW.
         =============================================================
         All eight carry `builder_mode = guided` and a structured spec —
         `filter_conditions`, `value_steps`, `record_source` — so the engine
         evaluates them NATIVELY on plain dicts and a payroll manager can open
         any of them in the Rule Composer and read what they do. The vendor
         catalogue is no longer a place python arrives from.

         The `filter_expression` and `python_code` fields are LEFT IN PLACE and
         are INERT: `_execute_single` routes a guided rule away from safe_eval
         before either is looked at. They stay as PROVENANCE — the next person
         to ask "is the sentence really what the legacy did?" can read both and
         compare, and `test_rule_composer.py` does exactly that for all eight.

         The 19.0.1.60.0 migration is what reaches the rows that already exist,
         because `noupdate` lives in the DATABASE and Odoo never refreshes a
         frozen row whatever this file later says (W13.1).

         THE NAMESPACE, for the expressions that remain as provenance.
         `filter_expression` was evaluated by
         `hr.api.transformation.rule._execute_single` with exactly four names in
         scope: `rec` (the record's extracted_data dict), `env`, `datetime` and
         `date`. It is `rec`, NOT `record` — a filter written against `record`
         raises NameError, the engine's `except Exception: pass` swallows it,
         and the row is simply dropped from the aggregate. A whole feed would
         sum to zero with nothing in the log. Every expression below uses `rec`.

         THE DROPPED DATE FILTER. The legacy re-parsed each overtime record's
         `OT_Date` and re-checked it against the same window it had just asked
         the API for (:497-557). The endpoint is date-scoped by
         fromDate/toDate, so that second check could only ever remove records
         Zoho had already excluded — while silently dropping any record whose
         date string did not parse as dd-MM-yyyy. Dropped, deliberately; the
         window lives in the pull, which is the only place it can be enforced.

         THE TWO THAT USED TO BE PYTHON, AND WHY THEY ARE NOT ANY MORE.
         DEPCOUNT counts rows inside a TABULAR SECTION of one employee record,
         which no `count` over RECORDS could express — counting store rows
         answers 1 for an employee with four dependants. `record_source =
         nested` is that missing idea, so DEPCOUNT is an ordinary count with a
         table path. WORKEDHRS had to add an integer count of SECONDS to an
         "H:MM" string, in one payload, in two different units; `value_steps`
         is that missing idea, because steps inside one record are added and
         each one declares what its field CONTAINS. Two shapes the engine did
         not have, rather than two programs it needed. -->
